# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Environments
.venv/
.env

# Datasets and experiment outputs never go in git. Datasets are not versioned at all;
# instead each run fingerprints the splits it read (file count, total size, digest of
# the path/size listing) into runs/<experiment>/meta.json, so a checkpoint can always
# be traced to the data behind it. See data/fingerprint.py.
datasets/
runs/
exports/
*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.engine
# TensorRT serialised engines. `.engine` is the Jetson convention and was the only one
# listed; `scripts/bench_trt.py` writes `<name>.<tag>.plan`, so the four plans the
# pro6000 sweep left in `exports/` sat untracked and *unignored* -- 100 MB that a
# `git add -A` would have committed. Both extensions, because both are produced.
*.plan

# Everything under assets/, then the figures back in by name.
#
# This used to be a denylist -- mp4, mov, pdf -- and its own comment said what it was
# for: "`git add -A` in a hurry should not be able to publish a client's floor plan".
# A denylist cannot do that job. The material this repo actually handles is retail CCTV
# from customer stores, and one frame of it is a .png. `assets/retail_static_plate_cam01.png`
# sat untracked in the tree while the denylist covered three formats none of which it was.
#
# So the default is now "no", and the figures that belong to the README and the docs are
# listed below. Adding one more is a deliberate act -- `git add -f`, then a line here --
# and the moment in between is the point: a frame of a customer's shop floor cannot be
# un-published once it is in the history.
#
# The list has to match what is tracked, exactly. A `!` line for a figure that has since
# been deleted is a hole with that filename's shape in it, and `tests/test_assets_allowlist.py`
# fails on one in either direction.
assets/*
# Added 2026-08-28. The left panel is a customer's shop floor, so every person in it
# has their head and shoulders blurred before this frame ever existed as a file. The
# blur is the union of two independent instruments, because one was measurably not
# enough: the box head at 0.12 missed people standing at the back of the aisle, and a
# privacy mask that misses somebody has published them. The second is the difference
# against that camera's own static plate, which owes nothing to a detector.
!assets/demo_Taichung-cam10.gif
!assets/bev3d_mesh_people.png
!assets/hydranet_demo.gif
!assets/mesh_shop_preview.png
!assets/retail_bootstrap_fixture_split.jpg
!assets/retail_cctv_scene.gif
!assets/social_preview.png

# Editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Credentials. Keys live in ~/.gcp/, outside the repo; these patterns are a second
# line of defence so a stray copy cannot be committed by accident.
*-sa.json
*service-account*.json
*credentials*.json
gcp-key*.json
.gcp/

# Model release bundles. Staged locally, published to GCS -- a bundle carries a 128 MB
# checkpoint and its per-target builds, and git is not where those belong. MANIFEST.json
# is what makes a bundle verifiable wherever it lives; see docs/RELEASE.md.
releases/
